language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_014_solitaire_battleships: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The Battleships puzzle is played on a grid. The fleet has battleships (four squares long), cruisers (three squares long), destroyers (two squares long) and submarines (one square each). Ships lie horizontally or vertically, and no two ships occupy adjacent squares, not even diagonally. The digits along the right side of and below the grid give the number of squares in the corresponding rows and columns that are occupied by ships. Some puzzles start with shots that show water, a complete submarine, or part of a longer ship. Find the grid, where each cell is water, a submarine, or a ship part (left, right, top, bottom or middle).
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_014_solitaire_battleships.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_014_solitaire_battleships.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_014_solitaire_battleships_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_014_solitaire_battleships.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_014_solitaire_battleships_1.param -run-solver

$ rows, cols: number of rows and columns of the grid
given rows, cols : int(1..)
$ rowsum[r]: number of occupied squares in row r, indices 1-based
given rowsum : matrix indexed by [int(1..rows)] of int(0..cols)
$ colsum[c]: number of occupied squares in column c, indices 1-based
given colsum : matrix indexed by [int(1..cols)] of int(0..rows)
$ fleet_counts[k,1]: length of the k-th ship type (rows 1 to 4: length 4, 3, 2, 1); fleet_counts[k,2]: number of ships of that length
given fleet_counts : matrix indexed by [int(1..4), int(1..2)] of int(0..)
$ hints[h,1..3]: a shot, with its 0-based row, its 0-based column and the cell code it shows (the domain is wide enough for a row or column index)
given hints_dim1 : int(1..)
given hints : matrix indexed by [int(1..hints_dim1), int(1..3)] of int(0..rows+cols)

$ the cell codes: water, submarine (circle), and the ship parts left, right, top, bottom and middle
letting WATER = 0
letting CIRCLE = 2
letting LEFT = 3
letting RIGHT = 4
letting TOP = 5
letting BOTTOM = 6
letting MIDDLE = 7

$ cell codes of the grid, padded with a border of water so that every neighbour index is in range (auxiliary)
find p : matrix indexed by [int(0..rows+1), int(0..cols+1)] of int(0..7)
$ grid[r,c]: code of the cell in row r, column c, indices 1-based
find grid : matrix indexed by [int(1..rows), int(1..cols)] of int(0..7)

$ search hints
branching on [grid]
heuristic conflict

such that
$ the border around the grid is water
forAll c : int(0..cols+1) . (p[0,c] = WATER /\ p[rows+1,c] = WATER),
forAll r : int(1..rows) . (p[r,0] = WATER /\ p[r,cols+1] = WATER),
$ the output grid is the inside of the padded grid
forAll r : int(1..rows) . forAll c : int(1..cols) . (grid[r,c] = p[r,c]),
$ each shot fixes the cell it names (its row and column are 0-based)
forAll h : int(1..hints_dim1) . (p[hints[h,1]+1, hints[h,2]+1] = hints[h,3]),
$ the number of occupied squares in each row is the row count
forAll r : int(1..rows) . ((sum c : int(1..cols) . toInt(p[r,c] != WATER)) = rowsum[r]),
$ the number of occupied squares in each column is the column count
forAll c : int(1..cols) . ((sum r : int(1..rows) . toInt(p[r,c] != WATER)) = colsum[c]),
$ a submarine is a single square with water all around it
forAll r : int(1..rows) . forAll c : int(1..cols) . ((p[r,c] = CIRCLE) -> (p[r-1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c+1] = WATER)),
$ the left end of a horizontal ship has a middle or right part to its right, water to its left, and water on all other sides around it
forAll r : int(1..rows) . forAll c : int(1..cols) . ((p[r,c] = LEFT) -> (p[r,c-1] = WATER /\ (p[r,c+1] = MIDDLE \/ p[r,c+1] = RIGHT) /\ p[r-1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c+1] = WATER)),
$ the right end of a horizontal ship has a left or middle part to its left, water to its right, and water on all other sides around it
forAll r : int(1..rows) . forAll c : int(1..cols) . ((p[r,c] = RIGHT) -> (p[r,c+1] = WATER /\ (p[r,c-1] = LEFT \/ p[r,c-1] = MIDDLE) /\ p[r-1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c+1] = WATER)),
$ a middle part continues its ship either left and right or up and down, and no other ship square touches it, even diagonally
forAll r : int(1..rows) . forAll c : int(1..cols) . ((p[r,c] = MIDDLE) -> ((((p[r,c-1] = LEFT \/ p[r,c-1] = MIDDLE) /\ (p[r,c+1] = RIGHT \/ p[r,c+1] = MIDDLE) /\ p[r-1,c] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c] = WATER) \/ ((p[r-1,c] = TOP \/ p[r-1,c] = MIDDLE) /\ (p[r+1,c] = BOTTOM \/ p[r+1,c] = MIDDLE) /\ p[r,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r,c+1] = WATER)) /\ (p[r-1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c+1] = WATER))),
$ the top end of a vertical ship has a middle or bottom part below it, water above it, and water on all other sides around it
forAll r : int(1..rows) . forAll c : int(1..cols) . ((p[r,c] = TOP) -> (p[r-1,c] = WATER /\ (p[r+1,c] = BOTTOM \/ p[r+1,c] = MIDDLE) /\ p[r,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c+1] = WATER)),
$ the bottom end of a vertical ship has a top or middle part above it, water below it, and water on all other sides around it
forAll r : int(1..rows) . forAll c : int(1..cols) . ((p[r,c] = BOTTOM) -> (p[r+1,c] = WATER /\ (p[r-1,c] = TOP \/ p[r-1,c] = MIDDLE) /\ p[r,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r-1,c+1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c-1] = WATER /\ p[r+1,c+1] = WATER)),
$ the number of submarines is the count given for ships of length 1
(sum r : int(1..rows) . sum c : int(1..cols) . toInt(p[r,c] = CIRCLE)) = fleet_counts[4,2],
$ the number of destroyers (ships of length 2, horizontal or vertical) is the count given for length 2
((sum r : int(1..rows) . sum c : int(1..cols-1) . toInt((p[r,c] = LEFT) /\ (p[r,c+1] = RIGHT))) + (sum r : int(1..rows-1) . sum c : int(1..cols) . toInt((p[r,c] = TOP) /\ (p[r+1,c] = BOTTOM)))) = fleet_counts[3,2],
$ the number of cruisers (ships of length 3, horizontal or vertical) is the count given for length 3
((sum r : int(1..rows) . sum c : int(1..cols-2) . toInt((p[r,c] = LEFT) /\ (p[r,c+2] = RIGHT))) + (sum r : int(1..rows-2) . sum c : int(1..cols) . toInt((p[r,c] = TOP) /\ (p[r+2,c] = BOTTOM)))) = fleet_counts[2,2],
$ the number of battleships (ships of length 4, horizontal or vertical) is the count given for length 4
((sum r : int(1..rows) . sum c : int(1..cols-3) . toInt((p[r,c] = LEFT) /\ (p[r,c+3] = RIGHT))) + (sum r : int(1..rows-3) . sum c : int(1..cols) . toInt((p[r,c] = TOP) /\ (p[r+3,c] = BOTTOM)))) = fleet_counts[1,2]
